Regression command:
REQUIRE_AV1=1 ANDROID_SERIAL=<device-serial> ./native/ffmpeg/test-mediacodec-quality.sh

Fixture: deterministic H.264/yuv420p testsrc2, 1228x720, 30000/1001,
90 frames. The transcode path used MediaCodec H.264 hardware decode and
explicit planar `-pix_fmt yuv420p` MediaCodec encode. Host FFmpeg decoded the
source and output and calculated SSIM and PSNR after normalizing time bases.

Result:
hevc: component=c2.google.hevc.encoder SSIM=0.999304 PSNR=52.728940dB
av1: component=c2.google.av1.encoder SSIM=0.999240 PSNR=52.272864dB

Both cases exceeded the script defaults of SSIM 0.95 and PSNR 30 dB. The
script removed its uniquely named host and device temporary files on exit.

Related full-length diagnosis:
The user-supplied H.264 source was 1228x720, 1094 frames. The affected HEVC
output retained all frames and timestamps but measured SSIM 0.519492 and PSNR
14.278684 dB. Android reported a flexible/NV12 MediaCodec encoder ByteBuffer
layout with stride 1248 and slice-height 736 while FFmpeg wrote a tighter
layout. Forcing planar yuv420p removed the horizontal row displacement and
green band while retaining MediaCodec hardware decode and encode.
